n phase before execution",["Runtime","Related"]],["Programming/Time/Run/","source-block-3001","Runtime environments and virtual machines",["Runtime","Related"]],["Programming/Time/Run/","source-block-3047","Dynamic typing vs static typing",["Runtime","Related"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-0","Two's Complement",[]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-73","A mathematical operation on binary numbers and the most common method for representing signed integers in computing",["Two's Complement"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-192","Simplifies arithmetic operations and eliminates the issue of having two representations for zero",["Two's Complement"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-292","Overview",["Two's Complement"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-307","The most significant bit (MSB) indicates the sign of the number:",["Two's Complement","Overview"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-376","0 for positive numbers",["Two's Complement","Overview","The most significant bit (MSB) indicates the sign of the number:"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-406","1 for negative numbers",["Two's Complement","Overview","The most significant bit (MSB) indicates the sign of the number:"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-436","Positive numbers are represented as usual in binary",["Two's Complement","Overview"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-492","Negative numbers are represented by inverting all bits of their absolute value (one's complement) and adding one to the least significant bit (LSB)",["Two's Complement","Overview"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-644","Range",["Two's Complement"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-656","For an n-bit binary number:",["Two's Complement","Range"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-688","The range of representable integers is from -2^(n-1) to 2^(n-1) - 1",["Two's Complement","Range","For an n-bit binary number:"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-765","For example, an 8-bit system can represent integers from -128 to 127",["Two's Complement","Range","For an n-bit binary number:"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-843","Advantages",["Two's Complement"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-860","Single Zero Representation",["Two's Complement","Advantages"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-895","Unlike one's complement, which has both 00000000 and 11111111 representing zero, two's complement has a unique representation (00000000)",["Two's Complement","Advantages","Single Zero Representation"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1043","Simplified Arithmetic",["Two's Complement","Advantages"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1073","Addition, subtraction, and multiplication operations are the same for both signed and unsigned numbers",["Two's Complement","Advantages","Simplified Arithmetic"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1181","Simplifies hardware design",["Two's Complement","Advantages","Simplified Arithmetic"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1213","No need for separate signed and unsigned arithmetic circuits",["Two's Complement","Advantages","Simplified Arithmetic"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1279","Converting to Two's Complement",["Two's Complement"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1316","To find the two's complement of a binary number:",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1369","1. Invert all bits (one's complement)",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement","To find the two's complement of a binary number:"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1412","2. Add one to the LSB",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement","To find the two's complement of a binary number:"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1439","Example: Representing -5 in 8-bit",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1481","Start with the binary representation of 5: 00000101",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement","Example: Representing -5 in 8-bit"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1542","Invert all bits: 11111010",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement","Example: Representing -5 in 8-bit"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1575","Add one: 11111011",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement","Example: Representing -5 in 8-bit"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1600","Thus, -5 is represented as 11111011 in 8-bit two's complement",["Two's Complement","Converting to Two's Complement","Example: Representing -5 in 8-bit"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1671","Arithmetic Operations",["Two's Complement"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1699","Addition",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1716","Add the binary numbers directly",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations","Addition"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1753","If there's a carry out of the MSB, it's discarded",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations","Addition"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1808","Subtraction",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1828","Convert the number to be subtracted into its two's complement",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations","Subtraction"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-block-1895","Add it to the other number",["Two's Complement","Arithmetic Operations","Subtraction"]],["Programming/Two%27s%20Complement/","source-bl